Extra CA certificates for this installation
===========================================

Everything in this directory is copied to /usr/local/share/ca-certificates/ in
the image, and the following RUN update-ca-certificates registers it in the
container's trust store at build time.

Put PEM-encoded CA certificates here as *.crt files (not DER). Only *.crt is
picked up; this README is ignored. It exists so the directory survives in git
and the COPY in the Dockerfile always finds something to copy.


Why this exists
---------------

Odoo's auth_ldap builds "ldap://host:port" and calls start_tls_s() without
setting a single TLS option, so certificate validation relies entirely on this
trust store. An LDAP/AD server whose certificate was issued by an internal CA
therefore fails with:

    error:0A000086:SSL routines::certificate verify failed
    (unable to get local issuer certificate)

That is OpenSSL verify code 20 (issuer unknown), not code 18 (self-signed) —
worth distinguishing, because the two lead to different fixes.

The host's trust store does not apply inside the container. The host may verify
the very same connection successfully ("Verify return code: 0 (ok)") while the
container does not, which is what makes this failure confusing to diagnose.

Note that this cannot be fixed at runtime for good: a certificate copied into a
running container lives in its writable layer. It survives docker restart and a
reboot, but not the next update — the container is removed and recreated there.


Keeping it in sync with the host
--------------------------------

When the CA is already installed on the host, do not maintain a second copy.
Let the update run refresh this directory on every build, in docker2update.yaml:

    pre_build_files:
      - source: "/usr/local/share/ca-certificates"
        target: "."

The host store stays the single source of truth, and a CA added there later
reaches the image on the next update by itself. A missing source is a hard
error that aborts the run, so the CA cannot silently vanish from a build.


Verifying
---------

    docker exec <container> sh -c 'ls /usr/local/share/ca-certificates/; \
        ls /etc/ssl/certs/ | grep -i <ca-name>'
    docker exec <container> python3 -c \
        "import ldap; c=ldap.initialize('ldap://HOST:389'); \
         c.start_tls_s(); print('STARTTLS OK')"

The second command reproduces exactly what auth_ldap does.
